          ],
          "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.875,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "15"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.138,
            0.9,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "**Case (iii) :** Here, the graph is either completely above the x-axis or completely below the x-axis. So, it does not cut the x-axis at any point (see Fig. 2.5)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.189,
            0.483,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.296,
                0.487,
                0.321,
                0.505
              ],
              "content": "(i)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.514,
            0.188,
            0.883,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.656,
                0.484,
                0.686,
                0.502
              ],
              "content": "(ii)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.456,
                0.513,
                0.542,
                0.533
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.544,
            0.735,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": "So, the quadratic polynomial $ax^{2} + bx + c$ has **no zero** in this case."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.57,
            0.901,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": "So, you can see geometrically that a quadratic polynomial can have either two distinct zeroes or two equal zeroes (i.e., one zero), or no zero. This also means that a polynomial of degree 2 has atmost two zeroes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.634,
            0.902,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": "Now, what do you expect the geometrical meaning of the zeroes of a cubic polynomial to be? Let us find out. Consider the cubic polynomial $x ^ { 3 } - 4 x .$ . To see what the graph of $y = x ^ { 3 } - 4 \\underline { { x } }$ looks like, let us list a few values of y corresponding to a few values for x as shown in Table 2.2."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.751,
            0.792,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "| x | -2 | -1 | 0 | 1 | 2 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| y = x<sup>3</sup> - 4x | 0 | 3 | 0 | -3 | 0 |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.447,
                0.727,
                0.551,
                0.745
              ],
              "content": "Table 2.2"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_table_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.859,
            0.9,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "Locating the points of the table on a graph paper and drawing the graph, we see that the graph of $y = x ^ { 3 } - 4 x$ actually looks like the one given in Fig. 2.6."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
